nodecode-mdvault is a rewrite of kailashrajh's kailashrajh/hermes-mdvault:

  https://github.com/kailashrajh/hermes-mdvault
  commit 46de6b47ab0772a7a6566f362304c41e1768fa6b

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Kailash Rajh (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the whole memory provider, in Common Lisp against the Nodecode
cell contract. The vault engine came across as it reads in the original: the same
frontmatter reader (title, tags, the inline #tag scan), the same heading-split
sections, the same tokenizer shape (Latin words split on punctuation, CJK runs
indexed as character bigrams, stopwords dropped), the same bm25 ranking with
k1=1.2 and b=0.75, the same field weights (title 2.2, tag 1.6, heading 0.7 of
idf), a whole-query phrase bonus and the same light recency factor, then path
order. The same four verbs answer: mdvault_search, mdvault_note, mdvault_recent
and mdvault_remember, with the same folder and tag filters, the same default
limits and the same note-writing rules -- a slugged file name, a numeric suffix
instead of an overwrite, frontmatter only when the body has none, and a folder
refused when it would leave the vault or land in an excluded one. The per-turn
recall moved to the seam Nodecode has: where the original's prefetch hook
returned a block for the host to append, this cell writes a `vault-memory'
harness section on the turn's own input event, so the notes ride the next
request; the block keeps the original's <vault_memory> wrapper, its budget and
its truncation.

What was left out and why: the mirror of the harness's own memory writes
(on_memory_write), because Nodecode keeps its durable memory elsewhere and has
no single write event this cell could fold into one note -- /mdvault remember and
mdvault_remember write notes directly instead. The bundled vault-notes skill
became the (help :mdvault) topic and the tool descriptions, which is where a
Nodecode model reads its guidance. The dashboard config panel and the
`hermes mdvault status|reindex` CLI are the section's setup panel and the
`/mdvault` command. Dropped from the tokenizer: NFKC normalisation and
diacritic folding (no unicode normalisation is bundled here), and the
four-character prefix credit, which only widened a search beyond the words it
was given. Kana folding survives.
